{"parentUuid":"a31febfa-4472-456c-a78d-ca2d8a9bd6a3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Q6hVdcXwYLBAwC4pR2bSwS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Honest diagnosis from the actual error output:\n\n**It's the connection, not the hook.** Every failure mode in the logs is a transport error:\n\n- `Connection to ssh.github.com closed by remote host`\n- `Connection reset by peer`\n- `ssh: connect to host ssh.github.com port 443: Undefined error: 0`\n\nThe hook isn't *causing* the failure — it's *amplifying* it. The hook does ~4 separate SSH sessions (v1 push, then 3 v2 branches, then user branch). With flaky SSH, each session is another chance to fail, and any one failure aborts the whole push. On a healthy network the hook adds maybe 10-15s of total latency, not minutes.\n\nEvidence: in the second attempt, the v1 push **succeeded** (`Syncing entire/checkpoints/v1 with remote.... done`) before v2 failed mid-stream. SSH was working for one connection and dying on the next.\n\n### My suggestion: HTTPS swap\n\nReasoning:\n- `--no-verify` reduces 4 SSH connections to 1, but if SSH is broken ~50% of the time, that 1 connection still has a coin flip of working. Doesn't fix root cause.\n- HTTPS uses a completely different transport (TCP/TLS instead of SSH/22 or SSH/443). Bypasses whatever's wrong with your SSH path entirely.\n- One connection, one transport, no hook surprises (the checkpoint hook will still try to push checkpoints over the new HTTPS remote — that's fine, same transport).\n\nCaveat: you need HTTPS auth set up. If `gh auth setup-git` was ever run, git uses your gh token automatically and it'll just work. If not, we'll hit a clean auth error and can pivot.\n\nWant me to try the HTTPS swap?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1205,"cache_read_input_tokens":159345,"output_tokens":2229,"server_tool_use":{"web_sea